      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, you've touched upon the hidden mysteries of the timber realm, where internal knots and hidden defects lurk like goblins in the shadows. In the world of structural engineering, these hidden imperfections can indeed affect the strength and load capacity of your Douglas Fir beams.\n\nTo account for these potential weak spots, engineers often apply a factor of safety (FoS) to their calculations. The FoS is a multiplier that provides a buffer to ensure that even if the wood has hidden defects, the structure will still be safe. For residential construction, a common FoS for timber structures is between 1.5 and 2.0, depending on the specific conditions and the importance of the structure.\n\nWhen calculating the maximum span and load capacity, you can divide the allowable stress of the wood by the FoS to get a more conservative estimate of the beam's capacity. This will help ensure that your loft addition remains sturdy and doesn't wobble like a goblin on a trampoline, even if the wood has some hidden imperfections.\n\nRemember, it's always best to consult with a structural engineer who can assess the specific conditions of your project and provide tailored advice to ensure the safety and stability of your loft addition.",
